Good — this is the map version of the car glitch rules we did. Textures "breaking" in FiveM is almost always one of these five, and they're all preventable:
1. The texture isn't there when the game looks for it
Every model points at a texture dictionary (a .ytd file). If the texture isn't embedded in the model's own dictionary or the .ytd isn't streamed with the map, the surface renders pink/checkered or invisible. Rule: textures travel with the model, always — never rely on a texture living somewhere else.
2. Textures too big → the whole city melts
This is the famous one: a few 4K textures on small objects blow the game's texture budget, and then everything goes blurry and muddy, even stock buildings. Rule: props get 512 or 1024 max, big building faces 2048 at most — and if players ever report "the map went blurry," a fat texture is suspect #1.
3. Wrong size or format
Textures must be power-of-two (256, 512, 1024…) and saved as DDS with compression (DXT1 for plain color, DXT5 when there's transparency). A random-sized PNG renamed into the pack will shimmer, distort, or fail to load.
4. No mipmaps
Mipmaps are the pre-shrunk copies of a texture for distance. Without them, surfaces sparkle and crawl as you drive toward them. Rule: generate mipmaps on export, every time — one checkbox saves this entire category.
5. No LODs
LODs are the low-detail versions for far away. Without them, objects pop in late or vanish — the "that sign wasn't there a second ago" break. Same fix as the cars: every placed object needs its LOD, and map files need their SLODs for the far view.
Where you stand right now: your Chicago/Atlanta/GeePee world is a config layer on the stock map, so there's literally nothing custom to break yet — these rules switch on the moment we add your first custom assets, like city welcome signs or Backroads road signs.
